Optimizer coordinates are unrestricted coefficients, log SDs and Fisher
correlations; supplied starts and reported estimates remain natural-scale.
\code{optim} minimizes the negative log likelihood. The backend status reports
that method's termination condition. \code{SANN} has no convergence test, and
a zero-iteration run is reported as not run; neither is labelled converged.
Newton methods may require curvature during optimization even with OPG
covariance. No method guarantees a global maximum.

Without explicit scales, BFGS uses the reciprocal initial panel-score
column norms, \code{1 / sqrt(colSums(score^2))}, for regression coefficients with
non-negligible initial scores. Other coordinates use
\code{1 / pmax(1, sqrt(colSums(score^2)))}; all scales are bounded below by
\code{sqrt(.Machine$double.eps)}. This respects coefficient units under rescaled
regressors and Linear outcomes. Fixed-GH panel binary selection with free
\code{cor_error} and otherwise default \code{optim} BFGS settings instead uses
\code{parscale = 1/sqrt(n_panels)} with \code{reltol = 1e-12}. Explicit \code{parscale} or
\code{reltol} prevents that automatic pair. The random-effects auxiliaries used
by \code{init = "default"} have fresh BFGS optimizer settings; the joint fit's
optimizer options do not control them. Numerical auxiliaries inherit the
equation's person-effect integration settings, and PLN also inherits the
observation settings. The internal Linear auxiliary uses an exact Gaussian
likelihood without quadrature. See \code{\link[=panel_select]{panel_select()}} for supplied-start
precedence and initialization fallback.

The fit's \code{control} keeps requested settings and \code{optimizer$control} records
effective backend options. \code{update()} retains the fitted rules and settings
unless explicitly replaced; a fresh \code{panel_control(...)} is a complete
control replacement, not a partial patch to the old control.
